{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EgUdK5y5WG1XsPvX2VuxkW","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Domain Requirements: Docker Sandbox for Ralph Loops\n     2→\n     3→## Problem Statement\n     4→\n     5→Ralph loops execute AI agents with `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, giving them unrestricted access to the host filesystem, network, and shell. While this is intentional for trusted automation, it means a runaway or hallucinating agent can read, modify, or delete files anywhere the user has access — not just within the project being worked on.\n     6→\n     7→Additionally, configuring credentials (API keys, Git auth, SSH keys) for different providers inside Docker containers is complex and fragile. Users should not need to understand Docker volume mounts or credential forwarding.\n     8→\n     9→## Architecture Overview\n    10→\n    11→The sandbox uses a **split execution model** with two distinct paths depending on the provider:\n    12→\n    13→```\n    14→┌───────────────────────────── Host ──────────────────────────────┐\n    15→│                                                                  │\n    16→│  Vista Server (:4444)                                            │\n    17→│    ├── Ralph Service (creates containers, manages loops)         │\n    18→│    ├── Sandbox Proxy (/api/sandbox/opencode/*)                   │\n    19→│    │     └── forwards to opencode serve                          │\n    20→│    └── Container Manager (lifecycle, cleanup)                    │\n    21→│                                                                  │\n    22→│  opencode serve (:4096)         ← managed by Vista               │\n    23→│    ├── All AI provider credentials (native host auth)            │\n    24→│    ├── Per-loop sessions                                         │\n    25→│    └── File ops via bind mount (shared with container)           │\n    26→│                                                                  │\n    27→│  Project Files (host filesystem)                                 │\n    28→│    └── /path/to/project/  ◄══════════ bind mount ══════════╗     │\n    29→│                                                             ║     │\n    30→└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╫─────┘\n    31→                                                              ║\n    32→┌─── Modified Claudebox Container (per loop) ─────────────────╨────┐\n    33→│                                                                   │\n    34→│  /workspace/  ← full project root mounted (rw)                   │\n    35→│    ├── .vista/features/{slug}/  ← feature specs accessible       │\n    36→│    ├── .vista/ralph/{slug}/     ← job directory (progress, logs)  │\n    37→│    ├── src/, tests/, etc.       ← full source tree                │\n    38→│    └── .git/                    ← commit/push capable             │\n    39→│                                                                   │\n    40→│  Execution Path A — Claude Code loops:                            │\n    41→│    loop.sh → claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions             │\n    42→│    (runs natively inside container, credentials mounted)          │\n    43→│                                                                   │\n    44→│  Execution Path B — OpenCode loops:                               │\n    45→│    loop.sh → HTTP call to host.docker.internal:4444               │\n    46→│              /api/sandbox/opencode/{session}/message               │\n    47→│    (opencode serve on host does AI work, file ops via mount)      │\n    48→│                                                                   │\n    49→│  Mounted credentials (claudebox pattern):                         │\n    50→│    ~/.claude/ (rw)      ← Claude Code auth (OAuth + session)     │\n    51→│    ~/.ssh/ (ro)         ← git push/pull over SSH                 │\n    52→│    ~/.gitconfig (ro)    ← git identity                           │\n    53→│    ~/.config/gh/ (ro)   ← GitHub CLI (optional)                  │\n    54→│                                                                   │\n    55→│  Pre-installed: git, node, claude-code, python, bash              │\n    56→│  NOT installed: opencode (runs on host via serve)                 │\n    57→└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n    58→```\n    59→\n    60→### Execution Flow — Claude Code Loop\n    61→\n    62→```\n    63→1. ralph_start(provider=claude, model=sonnet, feature=my-feature)\n    64→2. Vista creates container from modified claudebox image\n    65→3. Project root mounted to /workspace/ (full access)\n    66→4. Claude credentials mounted (~/.claude/, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)\n    67→5. loop.sh runs inside container:\n    68→     ├── Reads PROMPT_build.md + task.md + progress.txt\n    69→     ├── Pipes prompt to: claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions --model sonnet\n    70→     ├── Claude Code runs natively inside container\n    71→     ├── Agent reads/writes files in /workspace/ (sandboxed)\n    72→     ├── git push from inside container (SSH keys mounted)\n    73→     └── Next iteration\n    74→6. Container removed when loop completes\n    75→```\n    76→\n    77→### Execution Flow — OpenCode Loop\n    78→\n    79→```\n    80→1. ralph_start(provider=opencode, model=claude-sonnet-4, feature=my-feature)\n    81→2. Vista ensures opencode serve is running on host (:4096)\n    82→3. Vista creates container from modified claudebox image\n    83→4. Project root mounted to /workspace/ (full access)\n    84→5. Vista creates opencode serve session, stores session_id in job.json\n    85→6. loop.sh runs inside container:\n    86→     ├── Reads PROMPT_build.md + task.md + progress.txt\n    87→     ├── HTTP POST to host.docker.internal:4444/api/sandbox/opencode/{session}/message\n    88→     ├── Vista proxies to opencode serve on host\n    89→     ├── opencode serve does AI reasoning + file operations on host\n    90→     ├── File changes visible in container instantly (bind mount)\n    91→     ├── git push from inside container (SSH keys mounted)\n    92→     └── Next iteration\n    93→7. Vista closes opencode serve session\n    94→8. Container removed when loop completes\n    95→```\n    96→\n    97→## Users & Personas\n    98→\n    99→### Primary: Developer (Human User)\n   100→- Enables or disables sandbox via a single toggle in the Vista dashboard\n   101→- Expects containers to \"just work\" — no manual `docker run` commands\n   102→- Needs a way to verify credentials are correctly mounted before starting loops\n   103→\n   104→### Secondary: AI Coding Assistant (Claude Code)\n   105→- Calls `ralph_start` MCP tool, which now routes through the sandbox if enabled\n   106→- Unaware of Docker — the sandbox is transparent to the MCP tool interface\n   107→- Operates within whatever filesystem view the container provides\n   108→\n   109→### Tertiary: Vista Server\n   110→- Manages `opencode serve` lifecycle on the host\n   111→- Creates and tears down containers per loop\n   112→- Proxies OpenCode API calls from containers to host\n   113→- Handles orphan cleanup on startup after crashes\n   114→\n   115→## Business Objectives\n   116→\n   117→1. Prevent agents from accessing files outside the project during ralph loops\n   118→2. Eliminate credential complexity for OpenCode loops (host-side serve handles all auth)\n   119→3. Leverage claudebox as a proven base image for Claude Code execution\n   120→4. Keep the system transparent: sandbox is an infrastructure concern, not a workflow change\n   121→5. Ensure agents can always access feature specs and full project context\n   122→6. Maintain full bypass capability when sandbox is disabled\n   123→\n   124→## Success Metrics\n   125→\n   126→- Claude Code loops run successfully inside container with mounted credentials\n   127→- OpenCode loops execute via host-side serve with zero AI credentials in container\n   128→- Agents can read `.vista/features/{slug}/` specs from inside the container\n   129→- Agents can commit and push from inside the container (SSH keys mounted)\n   130→- Container-per-loop isolation prevents interference between concurrent loops\n   131→- Vista startup detects and cleans up orphaned containers from previous crashes\n   132→- `sandbox.enabled = false` runs loops identically to the current (pre-sandbox) behavior\n   133→\n   134→## Functional Requirements\n   135→\n   136→### Core Functionality\n   137→\n   138→#### Modified Claudebox Image\n   139→- Based on the claudebox Dockerfile (debian:bookworm, git, node, claude-code, python)\n   140→- UID/GID matching with host user (claudebox pattern: build args `USER_ID`, `GROUP_ID`)\n   141→- `DEVCONTAINER=true` environment variable for Claude Code compatibility\n   142→- Pre-installed: git, Node.js, Claude Code CLI, Python, bash, gh CLI\n   143→- NOT installed: OpenCode (runs on host via `opencode serve`)\n   144→\n   145→#### OpenCode Serve Management\n   146→- Vista starts `opencode serve` on the host when sandbox is enabled and OpenCode provider is used\n   147→- Runs on configurable port (default: 4096)\n   148→- Vista manages the process lifecycle (start, health check, restart on failure, stop on shutdown)\n   149→- Each ralph loop gets its own session on the serve instance\n   150→- Vista exposes a proxy endpoint that containers call to interact with `opencode serve`\n   151→\n   152→#### Container-Per-Loop\n   153→- Each ralph loop spawns its own container\n   154→- Container is removed when the loop completes (or is stopped)\n   155→- Concurrent loops run in separate containers (full isolation)\n   156→- Container naming: `vista-ralph-{job_id}`\n   157→\n   158→#### Project Root Mounting\n   159→- Default mount: entire project root → `/workspace/` (read-write)\n   160→- This ensures agents can always access:\n   161→  - `.vista/features/{slug}/` — feature specs, PRDs, architecture docs\n   162→  - `.vista/ralph/{slug}/` — job directory (progress, logs, prompts)\n   163→  - `src/`, `tests/`, etc. — full source tree\n   164→  - `.git/` — commit and push capability\n   165→- Optional access profiles can restrict this mount for specific use cases\n   166→\n   167→#### Credential Mounting (Claudebox Pattern)\n   168→- `~/.claude/` → `/home/{user}/.claude/` (rw) — Claude Code OAuth + session state\n   169→- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` → env var forwarding (if set on host)\n   170→- `~/.ssh/` → `/home/{user}/.ssh/` (ro) — git SSH authentication\n   171→- `~/.gitconfig` → `/home/{user}/.gitconfig` (ro) — git identity\n   172→- `~/.config/gh/` → `/home/{user}/.config/gh/` (ro) — GitHub CLI (optional)\n   173→- Credential sharing is configured via `settings.json` (not exposed in dashboard UI in this phase)\n   174→\n   175→#### Credential Validation\n   176→- Credential validation runs automatically at Vista startup when sandbox is enabled\n   177→- Results logged to Vista server log for diagnostics\n   178→- Also available via internal API (`POST /api/sandbox/credentials/validate`) for programmatic use\n   179→- Spins up a temporary container with current credential config\n   180→- Runs per-credential validation commands inside the container\n   181→- Reports per-credential pass/fail results in log output\n   182→- Tears down temp container after validation\n   183→\n   184→#### Path Translation\n   185→- Automatic host-to-Docker path conversion (e.g., `C:\\Users\\Grove\\.ssh` → `/c/Users/Grove/.ssh`)\n   186→- Platform detection determines translation strategy\n   187→- Applied transparently to all mount configurations\n   188→\n   189→#### Network Access\n   190→- Containers have network access by default (required for: AI API calls in Claude Code path, host communication in OpenCode path, git push/pull)\n   191→- Optional per-profile network disable for research-only scenarios\n   192→- OpenCode path REQUIRES network access to reach `host.docker.internal`\n   193→\n   194→#### Bypass Mode\n   195→- `sandbox.enabled = false` completely bypasses Docker\n   196→- Loops run as direct subprocesses (current behavior)\n   197→- All sandbox settings are preserved but inactive\n   198→\n   199→### User Workflows\n   200→\n   201→1. **Setup Flow**: User enables sandbox in settings → Vista builds Docker image (from claudebox base) → User toggles credentials → User clicks \"Test Credentials\" → All green → Ready\n   202→2. **Claude Code Loop**: User starts ralph loop with Claude → container created with project mount + Claude creds → `claude -p` runs inside container → output streams as normal\n   203→3. **OpenCode Loop**: User starts ralph loop with OpenCode → Vista ensures `opencode serve` is running → container created with project mount → loop script calls Vista proxy → OpenCode serve does AI work on host → file changes visible in container\n   204→4. **Debugging**: User disables sandbox → loops run directly → user re-enables when done debugging\n   205→\n   206→### Business Rules\n   207→\n   208→- Project root is always mounted at `/workspace/` (full access is the default)\n   209→- Feature specs (`.vista/features/`) must always be accessible to the agent\n   210→- The `.git` directory must be mounted read-write for commit/push operations\n   211→- Credential providers that fail detection are shown as \"not found\" in the UI, not as errors\n   212→- Docker must be installed and running for sandbox to work; if not, show clear error with instructions\n   213→- OpenCode loops require `opencode serve` on the host; Vista manages this automatically\n   214→- Container cleanup is automatic when loops finish or are stopped\n   215→\n   216→## Non-Functional Requirements\n   217→\n   218→- **Performance**: Container creation should complete within 30 seconds. Bind mount I/O on Windows (via WSL2) may be slower than native — this is acceptable.\n   219→- **Platform**: Windows (primary, via Docker Desktop + WSL2), Linux and macOS (secondary)\n   220→- **Reliability**: Container state is verified before each use. Orphan cleanup runs on every Vista startup.\n   221→- **Security**: SSH keys mounted read-only. No AI API keys stored in container images. Container runs as non-root user with matching UID/GID. OpenCode credentials never enter containers.\n   222→- **Compatibility**: No changes to MCP tool interface. `ralph_start` works identically whether sandbox is enabled or not. Existing standalone `ralph.py` CLI is unaffected.\n   223→\n   224→## Constraints & Dependencies\n   225→\n   226→- **Docker Desktop** must be installed for sandbox functionality. Vista does not install Docker.\n   227→- **WSL2 backend** is required for Docker Desktop on Windows (performance and Linux container support).\n   228→- **Claudebox Dockerfile** is the base for the container image. Vista maintains a modified version with any additional tooling needed.\n   229→- **OpenCode CLI** must be installed on the host for OpenCode loops (`opencode serve`). Not needed inside the container.\n   230→- **Existing ralph infrastructure**: Sandbox wraps the existing loop script execution — same templates, same provider invocations (for Claude), just executed inside a container.\n   231→- **Ralph MCP Tool dependency**: The `ralph-mcp-tool` feature must be implemented first. Sandbox integrates at `RalphService.create_job()`.\n   232→- **Settings system**: Extends `vista/settings.json` with a `sandbox` section under `ralph`.\n   233→\n   234→## User Experience Requirements\n   235→\n   236→- **Zero Docker knowledge required**: Users interact with a toggle, credential checkboxes, and a test button — never with Docker commands\n   237→- **Progressive disclosure**: Simple toggle to enable, sensible defaults, advanced customization available but not required\n   238→- **Feedback**: Clear status indicators for container health, credential validation results, and `opencode serve` status\n   239→- **Error recovery**: If Docker is not running, show actionable error. If container fails to start, show logs. If credential validation fails, show which credential and why.\n   240→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fe45d16-ab3a-4e41-9b98-035326f9b7c3","uuid":"65f325e4-9734-42f5-b6dc-c6f4b67cb6a7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Grove/OneDrive/Documents/PYTHON/claude_plugins/vista/.vista/features/docker-sandbox/domain-requirements.md","content":"# Domain Requirements: Docker Sandbox for Ralph Loops\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRalph loops execute AI agents with `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, giving them unrestricted access to the host filesystem, network, and shell. While this is intentional for trusted automation, it means a runaway or hallucinating agent can read, modify, or delete files anywhere the user has access — not just within the project being worked on.\n\nAdditionally, configuring credentials (API keys, Git auth, SSH keys) for different providers inside Docker containers is complex and fragile. Users should not need to understand Docker volume mounts or credential forwarding.\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\nThe sandbox uses a **split execution model** with two distinct paths depending on the provider:\n\n```\n┌───────────────────────────── Host ──────────────────────────────┐\n│                                                                  │\n│  Vista Server (:4444)                                            │\n│    ├── Ralph Service (creates containers, manages loops)         │\n│    ├── Sandbox Proxy (/api/sandbox/opencode/*)                   │\n│    │     └── forwards to opencode serve                          │\n│    └── Container Manager (lifecycle, cleanup)                    │\n│                                                                  │\n│  opencode serve (:4096)         ← managed by Vista               │\n│    ├── All AI provider credentials (native host auth)            │\n│    ├── Per-loop sessions                                         │\n│    └── File ops via bind mount (shared with container)           │\n│                                                                  │\n│  Project Files (host filesystem)                                 │\n│    └── /path/to/project/  ◄══════════ bind mount ══════════╗     │\n│                                                             ║     │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╫─────┘\n                                                              ║\n┌─── Modified Claudebox Container (per loop) ─────────────────╨────┐\n│                                                                   │\n│  /workspace/  ← full project root mounted (rw)                   │\n│    ├── .vista/features/{slug}/  ← feature specs accessible       │\n│    ├── .vista/ralph/{slug}/     ← job directory (progress, logs)  │\n│    ├── src/, tests/, etc.       ← full source tree                │\n│    └── .git/                    ← commit/push capable             │\n│                                                                   │\n│  Execution Path A — Claude Code loops:                            │\n│    loop.sh → claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions             │\n│    (runs natively inside container, credentials mounted)          │\n│                                                                   │\n│  Execution Path B — OpenCode loops:                               │\n│    loop.sh → HTTP call to host.docker.internal:4444               │\n│              /api/sandbox/opencode/{session}/message               │\n│    (opencode serve on host does AI work, file ops via mount)      │\n│                                                                   │\n│  Mounted credentials (claudebox pattern):                         │\n│    ~/.claude/ (rw)      ← Claude Code auth (OAuth + session)     │\n│    ~/.ssh/ (ro)         ← git push/pull over SSH                 │\n│    ~/.gitconfig (ro)    ← git identity                           │\n│    ~/.config/gh/ (ro)   ← GitHub CLI (optional)                  │\n│                                                                   │\n│  Pre-installed: git, node, claude-code, python, bash              │\n│  NOT installed: opencode (runs on host via serve)                 │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Execution Flow — Claude Code Loop\n\n```\n1. ralph_start(provider=claude, model=sonnet, feature=my-feature)\n2. Vista creates container from modified claudebox image\n3. Project root mounted to /workspace/ (full access)\n4. Claude credentials mounted (~/.claude/, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)\n5. loop.sh runs inside container:\n     ├── Reads PROMPT_build.md + task.md + progress.txt\n     ├── Pipes prompt to: claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions --model sonnet\n     ├── Claude Code runs natively inside container\n     ├── Agent reads/writes files in /workspace/ (sandboxed)\n     ├── git push from inside container (SSH keys mounted)\n     └── Next iteration\n6. Container removed when loop completes\n```\n\n### Execution Flow — OpenCode Loop\n\n```\n1. ralph_start(provider=opencode, model=claude-sonnet-4, feature=my-feature)\n2. Vista ensures opencode serve is running on host (:4096)\n3. Vista creates container from modified claudebox image\n4. Project root mounted to /workspace/ (full access)\n5. Vista creates opencode serve session, stores session_id in job.json\n6. loop.sh runs inside container:\n     ├── Reads PROMPT_build.md + task.md + progress.txt\n     ├── HTTP POST to host.docker.internal:4444/api/sandbox/opencode/{session}/message\n     ├── Vista proxies to opencode serve on host\n     ├── opencode serve does AI reasoning + file operations on host\n     ├── File changes visible in container instantly (bind mount)\n     ├── git push from inside container (SSH keys mounted)\n     └── Next iteration\n7. Vista closes opencode serve session\n8. Container removed when loop completes\n```\n\n## Users & Personas\n\n### Primary: Developer (Human User)\n- Enables or disables sandbox via a single toggle in the Vista dashboard\n- Expects containers to \"just work\" — no manual `docker run` commands\n- Needs a way to verify credentials are correctly mounted before starting loops\n\n### Secondary: AI Coding Assistant (Claude Code)\n- Calls `ralph_start` MCP tool, which now routes through the sandbox if enabled\n- Unaware of Docker — the sandbox is transparent to the MCP tool interface\n- Operates within whatever filesystem view the container provides\n\n### Tertiary: Vista Server\n- Manages `opencode serve` lifecycle on the host\n- Creates and tears down containers per loop\n- Proxies OpenCode API calls from containers to host\n- Handles orphan cleanup on startup after crashes\n\n## Business Objectives\n\n1. Prevent agents from accessing files outside the project during ralph loops\n2. Eliminate credential complexity for OpenCode loops (host-side serve handles all auth)\n3. Leverage claudebox as a proven base image for Claude Code execution\n4. Keep the system transparent: sandbox is an infrastructure concern, not a workflow change\n5. Ensure agents can always access feature specs and full project context\n6. Maintain full bypass capability when sandbox is disabled\n\n## Success Metrics\n\n- Claude Code loops run successfully inside container with mounted credentials\n- OpenCode loops execute via host-side serve with zero AI credentials in container\n- Agents can read `.vista/features/{slug}/` specs from inside the container\n- Agents can commit and push from inside the container (SSH keys mounted)\n- Container-per-loop isolation prevents interference between concurrent loops\n- Vista startup detects and cleans up orphaned containers from previous crashes\n- `sandbox.enabled = false` runs loops identically to the current (pre-sandbox) behavior\n\n## Functional Requirements\n\n### Core Functionality\n\n#### Modified Claudebox Image\n- Based on the claudebox Dockerfile (debian:bookworm, git, node, claude-code, python)\n- UID/GID matching with host user (claudebox pattern: build args `USER_ID`, `GROUP_ID`)\n- `DEVCONTAINER=true` environment variable for Claude Code compatibility\n- Pre-installed: git, Node.js, Claude Code CLI, Python, bash, gh CLI\n- NOT installed: OpenCode (runs on host via `opencode serve`)\n\n#### OpenCode Serve Management\n- Vista starts `opencode serve` on the host when sandbox is enabled and OpenCode provider is used\n- Runs on configurable port (default: 4096)\n- Vista manages the process lifecycle (start, health check, restart on failure, stop on shutdown)\n- Each ralph loop gets its own session on the serve instance\n- Vista exposes a proxy endpoint that containers call to interact with `opencode serve`\n\n#### Container-Per-Loop\n- Each ralph loop spawns its own container\n- Container is removed when the loop completes (or is stopped)\n- Concurrent loops run in separate containers (full isolation)\n- Container naming: `vista-ralph-{job_id}`\n\n#### Project Root Mounting\n- Default mount: entire project root → `/workspace/` (read-write)\n- This ensures agents can always access:\n  - `.vista/features/{slug}/` — feature specs, PRDs, architecture docs\n  - `.vista/ralph/{slug}/` — job directory (progress, logs, prompts)\n  - `src/`, `tests/`, etc. — full source tree\n  - `.git/` — commit and push capability\n- Optional access profiles can restrict this mount for specific use cases\n\n#### Credential Mounting (Claudebox Pattern)\n- `~/.claude/` → `/home/{user}/.claude/` (rw) — Claude Code OAuth + session state\n- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` → env var forwarding (if set on host)\n- `~/.ssh/` → `/home/{user}/.ssh/` (ro) — git SSH authentication\n- `~/.gitconfig` → `/home/{user}/.gitconfig` (ro) — git identity\n- `~/.config/gh/` → `/home/{user}/.config/gh/` (ro) — GitHub CLI (optional)\n- Credential sharing is configured via `settings.json` (not exposed in dashboard UI in this phase)\n\n#### Credential Validation\n- Credential validation runs automatically at Vista startup when sandbox is enabled\n- Results logged to Vista server log for diagnostics\n- Also available via internal API (`POST /api/sandbox/credentials/validate`) for programmatic use\n- Spins up a temporary container with current credential config\n- Runs per-credential validation commands inside the container\n- Reports per-credential pass/fail results in log output\n- Tears down temp container after validation\n\n#### Path Translation\n- Automatic host-to-Docker path conversion (e.g., `C:\\Users\\Grove\\.ssh` → `/c/Users/Grove/.ssh`)\n- Platform detection determines translation strategy\n- Applied transparently to all mount configurations\n\n#### Network Access\n- Containers have network access by default (required for: AI API calls in Claude Code path, host communication in OpenCode path, git push/pull)\n- Optional per-profile network disable for research-only scenarios\n- OpenCode path REQUIRES network access to reach `host.docker.internal`\n\n#### Bypass Mode\n- `sandbox.enabled = false` completely bypasses Docker\n- Loops run as direct subprocesses (current behavior)\n- All sandbox settings are preserved but inactive\n\n### User Workflows\n\n1. **Setup Flow**: User enables sandbox in settings → Vista builds Docker image (from claudebox base) → User toggles credentials → User clicks \"Test Credentials\" → All green → Ready\n2. **Claude Code Loop**: User starts ralph loop with Claude → container created with project mount + Claude creds → `claude -p` runs inside container → output streams as normal\n3. **OpenCode Loop**: User starts ralph loop with OpenCode → Vista ensures `opencode serve` is running → container created with project mount → loop script calls Vista proxy → OpenCode serve does AI work on host → file changes visible in container\n4. **Debugging**: User disables sandbox → loops run directly → user re-enables when done debugging\n\n### Business Rules\n\n- Project root is always mounted at `/workspace/` (full access is the default)\n- Feature specs (`.vista/features/`) must always be accessible to the agent\n- The `.git` directory must be mounted read-write for commit/push operations\n- Credential providers that fail detection are shown as \"not found\" in the UI, not as errors\n- Docker must be installed and running for sandbox to work; if not, show clear error with instructions\n- OpenCode loops require `opencode serve` on the host; Vista manages this automatically\n- Container cleanup is automatic when loops finish or are stopped\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n- **Performance**: Container creation should complete within 30 seconds. Bind mount I/O on Windows (via WSL2) may be slower than native — this is acceptable.\n- **Platform**: Windows (primary, via Docker Desktop + WSL2), Linux and macOS (secondary)\n- **Reliability**: Container state is verified before each use. Orphan cleanup runs on every Vista startup.\n- **Security**: SSH keys mounted read-only. No AI API keys stored in container images. Container runs as non-root user with matching UID/GID. OpenCode credentials never enter containers.\n- **Compatibility**: No changes to MCP tool interface. `ralph_start` works identically whether sandbox is enabled or not. Existing standalone `ralph.py` CLI is unaffected.\n\n## Constraints & Dependencies\n\n- **Docker Desktop** must be installed for sandbox functionality. Vista does not install Docker.\n- **WSL2 backend** is required for Docker Desktop on Windows (performance and Linux container support).\n- **Claudebox Dockerfile** is the base for the container image. Vista maintains a modified version with any additional tooling needed.\n- **OpenCode CLI** must be installed on the host for OpenCode loops (`opencode serve`). Not needed inside the container.\n- **Existing ralph infrastructure**: Sandbox wraps the existing loop script execution — same templates, same provider invocations (for Claude), just executed inside a container.\n- **Ralph MCP Tool dependency**: The `ralph-mcp-tool` feature must be implemented first. Sandbox integrates at `RalphService.create_job()`.\n- **Settings system**: Extends `vista/settings.json` with a `sandbox` section under `ralph`.\n\n## User Experience Requirements\n\n- **Zero Docker knowledge required**: Users interact with a toggle, credential checkboxes, and a test button — never with Docker commands\n- **Progressive disclosure**: Simple toggle to enable, sensible defaults, advanced customization available but not required\n- **Feedback**: Clear status indicators for container health, credential validation results, and `opencode serve` status\n- **Error recovery**: If Docker is not running, show actionable error. If container fails to start, show logs. If credential validation fails, show which credential and why.\n","numLines":240,"startLine":1,"totalLines":240}}}
