{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YSfhqrNqSoLsV7rDD96wbm","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Spec: Access Profiles\n     2→\n     3→## Problem Statement\n     4→\n     5→While the default sandbox behavior mounts the full project root (giving agents complete project access including feature specs), some use cases benefit from restricting what the agent can see. A cheap model doing research might only need read-only access, while an expensive model needs full read-write. Named profiles provide this flexibility without changing the default full-access behavior.\n     6→\n     7→## Proposed Solution\n     8→\n     9→Named **access profiles** stored in settings, each defining a set of directory mounts with access modes and an optional network policy override. The default profile is `full-access` (entire project root, read-write), which ensures agents can always reference feature specs, source code, and the full project context.\n    10→\n    11→> **Current phase:** Profiles are a configuration-level concept managed via `settings.json`. They are not exposed in the dashboard UI. The dashboard provides only a sandbox enable/disable toggle. Power users can define custom profiles by editing `settings.json` directly.\n    12→\n    13→### Profile Definition\n    14→\n    15→```python\n    16→@dataclass\n    17→class MountSpec:\n    18→    path: str        # Relative to project root, supports ${FEATURE} variable\n    19→    mode: str        # \"rw\" or \"ro\"\n    20→\n    21→@dataclass\n    22→class AccessProfile:\n    23→    name: str\n    24→    mounts: list[MountSpec]\n    25→    network: bool | None  # None = inherit global setting\n    26→```\n    27→\n    28→### Built-In Profiles\n    29→\n    30→#### `full-access` (DEFAULT)\n    31→Full project directory, read-write. The agent sees everything in the project including feature specs, architecture docs, source code, tests, and git history.\n    32→\n    33→```json\n    34→{\n    35→  \"mounts\": [\n    36→    { \"path\": \".\", \"mode\": \"rw\" }\n    37→  ]\n    38→}\n    39→```\n    40→\n    41→**Mounted as:** project root → `/workspace/`\n    42→\n    43→This is the default for all loops. Agents can always access:\n    44→- `.vista/features/{slug}/` — feature specs, PRDs, architecture docs\n    45→- `.vista/ralph/{slug}/` — job directories, progress files\n    46→- `src/`, `tests/`, etc. — full source tree\n    47→- `.git/` — commit and push capability\n    48→- `CLAUDE.md` — project instructions\n    49→\n    50→#### `feature-scoped`\n    51→Only the feature directory, source code, and git. Agent cannot see other features, configs outside src, etc.\n    52→\n    53→```json\n    54→{\n    55→  \"mounts\": [\n    56→    { \"path\": \".vista/features/${FEATURE}\", \"mode\": \"rw\" },\n    57→    { \"path\": \".vista/ralph/${FEATURE}\", \"mode\": \"rw\" },\n    58→    { \"path\": \"src\", \"mode\": \"rw\" },\n    59→    { \"path\": \"tests\", \"mode\": \"rw\" },\n    60→    { \"path\": \".git\", \"mode\": \"rw\" }\n    61→  ]\n    62→}\n    63→```\n    64→\n    65→**Mounted as:** Each path becomes a subdirectory under `/workspace/`. Only these subdirectories exist.\n    66→\n    67→**Variable substitution:** `${FEATURE}` is replaced with the feature/slug name at loop start time.\n    68→\n    69→#### `research-only`\n    70→Full project, read-only. No writes, no network. For exploratory/analytical loops.\n    71→\n    72→```json\n    73→{\n    74→  \"mounts\": [\n    75→    { \"path\": \".\", \"mode\": \"ro\" }\n    76→  ],\n    77→  \"network\": false\n    78→}\n    79→```\n    80→\n    81→**Note:** `research-only` with `network: false` will NOT work for OpenCode loops, since those require network access to reach `host.docker.internal`. It works for Claude Code loops only (Claude Code runs natively in the container with `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` mounted).\n    82→\n    83→### Profile Resolution\n    84→\n    85→When a ralph loop starts, the profile is resolved through this priority chain:\n    86→\n    87→```\n    88→1. Explicit override (user/LLM specifies profile in ralph_start)\n    89→   ↓ (if not specified)\n    90→2. Model default (from sandbox.model_defaults)\n    91→   ↓ (if model not in defaults)\n    92→3. Fallback: \"full-access\"\n    93→```\n    94→\n    95→```python\n    96→def resolve_profile(explicit: str | None, model: str, settings: dict) -> AccessProfile:\n    97→    if explicit:\n    98→        return settings[\"sandbox\"][\"profiles\"][explicit]\n    99→\n   100→    model_defaults = settings[\"sandbox\"].get(\"model_defaults\", {})\n   101→    if model in model_defaults:\n   102→        return settings[\"sandbox\"][\"profiles\"][model_defaults[model]]\n   103→\n   104→    return settings[\"sandbox\"][\"profiles\"][\"full-access\"]\n   105→```\n   106→\n   107→### Model Defaults\n   108→\n   109→Maps model names to profile names:\n   110→\n   111→```json\n   112→{\n   113→  \"sandbox\": {\n   114→    \"model_defaults\": {\n   115→      \"haiku\": \"feature-scoped\",\n   116→      \"sonnet\": \"full-access\",\n   117→      \"opus\": \"full-access\"\n   118→    }\n   119→  }\n   120→}\n   121→```\n   122→\n   123→Models not listed fall back to `full-access`.\n   124→\n   125→### Variable Substitution\n   126→\n   127→Profile mount paths can contain variables that are resolved at loop start time:\n   128→\n   129→| Variable | Resolved To | Example |\n   130→|----------|-------------|---------|\n   131→| `${FEATURE}` | Feature name / slug | `my-feature` |\n   132→| `${PROJECT_ROOT}` | Absolute project root path | `/workspace` (always this inside container) |\n   133→\n   134→### Mount Translation to Docker Volumes\n   135→\n   136→Each `MountSpec` is translated to a Docker bind mount:\n   137→\n   138→```python\n   139→def to_docker_mount(spec: MountSpec, project_root: Path, translator: PathTranslator) -> dict:\n   140→    host_path = project_root / spec.path  # Resolve relative to project\n   141→    docker_path = translator.translate(str(host_path))  # Platform translation\n   142→    container_path = f\"/workspace/{spec.path}\"  # Always under /workspace\n   143→\n   144→    return {\n   145→        \"host\": docker_path,\n   146→        \"container\": container_path,\n   147→        \"mode\": spec.mode,\n   148→    }\n   149→```\n   150→\n   151→**Special case for `\".\"`**: When path is `.`, mount the entire project root as `/workspace/`.\n   152→\n   153→### Validation Rules\n   154→\n   155→- A profile must have at least one mount\n   156→- Mount paths must be relative (no absolute paths or `..` traversal)\n   157→- Mount paths must resolve to locations within the project root\n   158→- If a mount path doesn't exist on the host at loop start time, it is skipped with a warning (not an error — the feature directory might not exist yet)\n   159→- Profile names must be alphanumeric + hyphens, no spaces\n   160→- `\"rw\"` and `\"ro\"` are the only valid modes\n   161→\n   162→### Network Policy\n   163→\n   164→Each profile can optionally override the global network policy:\n   165→\n   166→```json\n   167→{\n   168→  \"research-only\": {\n   169→    \"mounts\": [...],\n   170→    \"network\": false\n   171→  },\n   172→  \"full-access\": {\n   173→    \"mounts\": [...]\n   174→    // No \"network\" key: inherits global setting\n   175→  }\n   176→}\n   177→```\n   178→\n   179→Resolution: `profile.network ?? global_network_setting`\n   180→\n   181→**Constraint:** OpenCode sandbox loops always require network access (to reach `host.docker.internal`). If a profile disables network and the loop uses OpenCode, the loop will fail with a clear error: \"OpenCode sandbox loops require network access. Profile '{name}' has network disabled.\"\n   182→\n   183→## Edge Cases\n   184→\n   185→- **Mount path doesn't exist**: Skipped with warning. Agent sees the directory as missing inside the container.\n   186→- **Overlapping mounts**: If both `.` (full project) and `src/` are mounted, Docker handles this correctly.\n   187→- **`${FEATURE}` not provided**: If a profile uses `${FEATURE}` but the loop doesn't specify a feature name, error returned to caller.\n   188→- **Profile deleted while container running**: Container continues with current mounts. Container is per-loop so it's removed when loop finishes anyway.\n   189→- **Empty mount list**: Profile validation rejects this at settings save time.\n   190→- **`research-only` + OpenCode**: Fails with clear error (network required for OpenCode path).\n   191→\n   192→## Dependencies\n   193→\n   194→- Spec: [container-lifecycle](container-lifecycle.md) - Consumes profiles to create containers with correct mounts\n   195→- Spec: [settings](settings.md) - Profiles stored in `ralph.sandbox.profiles`\n   196→- Spec: [dashboard-ui](dashboard-ui.md) - Sandbox toggle only (no profile editor in this phase)\n   197→\n   198→## Testing Strategy\n   199→\n   200→- Unit tests for profile resolution (explicit > model default > fallback)\n   201→- Unit tests for variable substitution (`${FEATURE}`, `${PROJECT_ROOT}`)\n   202→- Unit tests for mount translation to Docker volumes\n   203→- Unit tests for validation rules (reject invalid paths, require at least one mount)\n   204→- Unit tests for network policy resolution (override vs inherit)\n   205→- Unit test: OpenCode + network-disabled profile fails with clear error\n   206→- Integration test: container with `full-access` profile can access all project files\n   207→- Integration test: container with `research-only` profile rejects writes\n   208→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14b921d9-eeaf-453d-9533-337d6f497996","uuid":"d91c5e0c-799e-4fc7-92d8-270e4f0fa1ab","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Grove/OneDrive/Documents/PYTHON/claude_plugins/vista/.vista/features/docker-sandbox/specs/access-profiles.md","content":"# Spec: Access Profiles\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nWhile the default sandbox behavior mounts the full project root (giving agents complete project access including feature specs), some use cases benefit from restricting what the agent can see. A cheap model doing research might only need read-only access, while an expensive model needs full read-write. Named profiles provide this flexibility without changing the default full-access behavior.\n\n## Proposed Solution\n\nNamed **access profiles** stored in settings, each defining a set of directory mounts with access modes and an optional network policy override. The default profile is `full-access` (entire project root, read-write), which ensures agents can always reference feature specs, source code, and the full project context.\n\n> **Current phase:** Profiles are a configuration-level concept managed via `settings.json`. They are not exposed in the dashboard UI. The dashboard provides only a sandbox enable/disable toggle. Power users can define custom profiles by editing `settings.json` directly.\n\n### Profile Definition\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass MountSpec:\n    path: str        # Relative to project root, supports ${FEATURE} variable\n    mode: str        # \"rw\" or \"ro\"\n\n@dataclass\nclass AccessProfile:\n    name: str\n    mounts: list[MountSpec]\n    network: bool | None  # None = inherit global setting\n```\n\n### Built-In Profiles\n\n#### `full-access` (DEFAULT)\nFull project directory, read-write. The agent sees everything in the project including feature specs, architecture docs, source code, tests, and git history.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mounts\": [\n    { \"path\": \".\", \"mode\": \"rw\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Mounted as:** project root → `/workspace/`\n\nThis is the default for all loops. Agents can always access:\n- `.vista/features/{slug}/` — feature specs, PRDs, architecture docs\n- `.vista/ralph/{slug}/` — job directories, progress files\n- `src/`, `tests/`, etc. — full source tree\n- `.git/` — commit and push capability\n- `CLAUDE.md` — project instructions\n\n#### `feature-scoped`\nOnly the feature directory, source code, and git. Agent cannot see other features, configs outside src, etc.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mounts\": [\n    { \"path\": \".vista/features/${FEATURE}\", \"mode\": \"rw\" },\n    { \"path\": \".vista/ralph/${FEATURE}\", \"mode\": \"rw\" },\n    { \"path\": \"src\", \"mode\": \"rw\" },\n    { \"path\": \"tests\", \"mode\": \"rw\" },\n    { \"path\": \".git\", \"mode\": \"rw\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Mounted as:** Each path becomes a subdirectory under `/workspace/`. Only these subdirectories exist.\n\n**Variable substitution:** `${FEATURE}` is replaced with the feature/slug name at loop start time.\n\n#### `research-only`\nFull project, read-only. No writes, no network. For exploratory/analytical loops.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mounts\": [\n    { \"path\": \".\", \"mode\": \"ro\" }\n  ],\n  \"network\": false\n}\n```\n\n**Note:** `research-only` with `network: false` will NOT work for OpenCode loops, since those require network access to reach `host.docker.internal`. It works for Claude Code loops only (Claude Code runs natively in the container with `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` mounted).\n\n### Profile Resolution\n\nWhen a ralph loop starts, the profile is resolved through this priority chain:\n\n```\n1. Explicit override (user/LLM specifies profile in ralph_start)\n   ↓ (if not specified)\n2. Model default (from sandbox.model_defaults)\n   ↓ (if model not in defaults)\n3. Fallback: \"full-access\"\n```\n\n```python\ndef resolve_profile(explicit: str | None, model: str, settings: dict) -> AccessProfile:\n    if explicit:\n        return settings[\"sandbox\"][\"profiles\"][explicit]\n\n    model_defaults = settings[\"sandbox\"].get(\"model_defaults\", {})\n    if model in model_defaults:\n        return settings[\"sandbox\"][\"profiles\"][model_defaults[model]]\n\n    return settings[\"sandbox\"][\"profiles\"][\"full-access\"]\n```\n\n### Model Defaults\n\nMaps model names to profile names:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sandbox\": {\n    \"model_defaults\": {\n      \"haiku\": \"feature-scoped\",\n      \"sonnet\": \"full-access\",\n      \"opus\": \"full-access\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nModels not listed fall back to `full-access`.\n\n### Variable Substitution\n\nProfile mount paths can contain variables that are resolved at loop start time:\n\n| Variable | Resolved To | Example |\n|----------|-------------|---------|\n| `${FEATURE}` | Feature name / slug | `my-feature` |\n| `${PROJECT_ROOT}` | Absolute project root path | `/workspace` (always this inside container) |\n\n### Mount Translation to Docker Volumes\n\nEach `MountSpec` is translated to a Docker bind mount:\n\n```python\ndef to_docker_mount(spec: MountSpec, project_root: Path, translator: PathTranslator) -> dict:\n    host_path = project_root / spec.path  # Resolve relative to project\n    docker_path = translator.translate(str(host_path))  # Platform translation\n    container_path = f\"/workspace/{spec.path}\"  # Always under /workspace\n\n    return {\n        \"host\": docker_path,\n        \"container\": container_path,\n        \"mode\": spec.mode,\n    }\n```\n\n**Special case for `\".\"`**: When path is `.`, mount the entire project root as `/workspace/`.\n\n### Validation Rules\n\n- A profile must have at least one mount\n- Mount paths must be relative (no absolute paths or `..` traversal)\n- Mount paths must resolve to locations within the project root\n- If a mount path doesn't exist on the host at loop start time, it is skipped with a warning (not an error — the feature directory might not exist yet)\n- Profile names must be alphanumeric + hyphens, no spaces\n- `\"rw\"` and `\"ro\"` are the only valid modes\n\n### Network Policy\n\nEach profile can optionally override the global network policy:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"research-only\": {\n    \"mounts\": [...],\n    \"network\": false\n  },\n  \"full-access\": {\n    \"mounts\": [...]\n    // No \"network\" key: inherits global setting\n  }\n}\n```\n\nResolution: `profile.network ?? global_network_setting`\n\n**Constraint:** OpenCode sandbox loops always require network access (to reach `host.docker.internal`). If a profile disables network and the loop uses OpenCode, the loop will fail with a clear error: \"OpenCode sandbox loops require network access. Profile '{name}' has network disabled.\"\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- **Mount path doesn't exist**: Skipped with warning. Agent sees the directory as missing inside the container.\n- **Overlapping mounts**: If both `.` (full project) and `src/` are mounted, Docker handles this correctly.\n- **`${FEATURE}` not provided**: If a profile uses `${FEATURE}` but the loop doesn't specify a feature name, error returned to caller.\n- **Profile deleted while container running**: Container continues with current mounts. Container is per-loop so it's removed when loop finishes anyway.\n- **Empty mount list**: Profile validation rejects this at settings save time.\n- **`research-only` + OpenCode**: Fails with clear error (network required for OpenCode path).\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Spec: [container-lifecycle](container-lifecycle.md) - Consumes profiles to create containers with correct mounts\n- Spec: [settings](settings.md) - Profiles stored in `ralph.sandbox.profiles`\n- Spec: [dashboard-ui](dashboard-ui.md) - Sandbox toggle only (no profile editor in this phase)\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n- Unit tests for profile resolution (explicit > model default > fallback)\n- Unit tests for variable substitution (`${FEATURE}`, `${PROJECT_ROOT}`)\n- Unit tests for mount translation to Docker volumes\n- Unit tests for validation rules (reject invalid paths, require at least one mount)\n- Unit tests for network policy resolution (override vs inherit)\n- Unit test: OpenCode + network-disabled profile fails with clear error\n- Integration test: container with `full-access` profile can access all project files\n- Integration test: container with `research-only` profile rejects writes\n","numLines":208,"startLine":1,"totalLines":208}}}
